# Violence as Evidence of Insecure Formality

## A Formal Article

---

## Abstract

This article argues that violence is not merely a moral failure or a political tool, but a **structural symptom**—evidence that a formal system is insecure. Insecurity, in this context, is the condition of a system that cannot ground itself, cannot secure consent, cannot demonstrate fidelity, and therefore must resort to force to persist. Violence, on this account, is the **manifestation of formal insecurity** in the domain of practice. The article proceeds by (1) defining formality and insecurity, (2) establishing the logical relationship between insecurity and coercion, (3) demonstrating the historical pattern, (4) formalizing the argument in Lean 4, and (5) drawing the implications for logic, ethics, and politics.

---

## 1. Introduction

Formal systems—logics, institutions, ideologies, governments—present themselves as **secure**.

- They claim universality.
- They claim necessity.
- They claim objectivity.
- They claim legitimacy.

But these claims are not always matched by reality. A formal system may claim to be secure while being **insecure**—unable to ground itself, unable to secure consent, unable to demonstrate fidelity.

**Violence is the evidence of this insecurity.**

When a formal system cannot ground itself, it resorts to force to maintain its claims. When it cannot secure consent, it resorts to coercion. When it cannot demonstrate fidelity, it resorts to suppression.

**Violence is not the proof of strength. It is the proof of insecurity.**

This article details the argument formally.

